        [: `Duration`]
        [: `Duration`]
        [: no]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `clamp(duration, min, max)`]
        [: `Duration, Duration, Duration`]
        [: `Duration`]
        [: no]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `duration_add(left, right)`]
        [: `Duration, Duration`]
        [: `Duration`]
        [: no]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `duration_subtract(left, right)`]
        [: `Duration, Duration`]
        [: `Duration`]
        [: no]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `duration_scale(duration, factor)`]
        [: `Duration, Float`]
        [: `Duration`]
        [: no]
    ]
]

`duration_add(left, right)` returns the sum of `left` and `right`. `duration_subtract(left, right)` returns `left` minus `right`. `duration_scale(duration, factor)` returns the result of scaling `duration` by `factor`.

### Timestamp helpers

[table:
    [th:
        [: Function]
        [: Parameters]
        [: Returns]
        [: Error]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `unix_seconds(timestamp)`]
        [: `Timestamp`]
        [: `Float`]
        [: no]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `unix_milliseconds(timestamp)`]
        [: `Timestamp`]
        [: `Float`]
        [: no]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `to_iso_string(timestamp)`]
        [: `Timestamp`]
        [: `String, Error!`]
        [: yes]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `timestamp_offset(timestamp, duration)`]
        [: `Timestamp, Duration`]
        [: `Timestamp`]
        [: no]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `timestamp_difference(start, end)`]
        [: `Timestamp, Timestamp`]
        [: `Duration`]
        [: no]
    ]
]

`timestamp_offset(timestamp, duration)` returns the instant `duration` away from `timestamp`. `timestamp_difference(start, end)` returns the duration from `start` to `end`, mirroring `duration_between`.

### Semantic contract

`Timestamp` counts milliseconds from the Unix epoch, 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, and always denotes a UTC instant. `Duration` is a signed millisecond quantity. `TimeMark` is a point on a monotonic clock whose origin is unspecified and unobservable. Marks are meaningful only as arguments to `elapsed_since` and `duration_between`. A mark never decreases within one program run, and both functions return a non-negative duration when the earlier mark is passed first.

`timestamp_from_iso_string` accepts exactly one text format:

- a calendar date `YYYY-MM-DD`
- the separator `T`
- a time of day `HH:MM:SS`
- an optional fractional second written as `.` followed by one to nine digits
- a mandatory UTC designator, either `Z` or a numeric offset `+HH:MM` or `-HH:MM`

Text that does not match returns through `Error!`. This rejects a missing designator, a lowercase `t` or `z`, surrounding whitespace, a date-only string and any other calendar or locale convention.

Field values are validated: month 1 to 12, a day that exists in that month of that year, hour 0 to 23 and minute and second 0 to 59. An out-of-range field returns through `Error!` instead of rolling over into the next day or month. Leap seconds are not accepted.

Fractional seconds resolve to milliseconds. Digits beyond the third are truncated toward zero, never rounded.

`to_iso_string` renders the same format with a `Z` designator and exactly three fractional digits. Rendering requires an instant within 8 640 000 000 000 000 milliseconds of the epoch, roughly 273 790 years either side. An instant outside that range returns through `Error!`. A fractional millisecond is truncated toward zero before rendering.

Duration arithmetic is exact addition, subtraction and multiplication of millisecond quantities. Because the arithmetic functions return opaque values, a result that leaves the finite `Float` range is not rejected where the arithmetic happens: it is rejected when the duration is read as a `Float` by `as_seconds` or `as_milliseconds`, at the shared external `Float` boundary.

Every `Float` reaching this package is already finite, because `NaN` and the infinities are never valid Moth `Float` values, so the package validates no argument of its own. The four `Float`-returning accessors `as_seconds`, `as_milliseconds`, `unix_seconds` and `unix_milliseconds` are checked by that shared boundary.

`Duration`, `TimeMark` and `Timestamp` support no equality, no ordering and no use as map keys. Compare durations through `as_milliseconds` and timestamps through `unix_milliseconds`.

Every failure in this package is observable in Moth: either a returned `Error!` or the shared `Float` boundary. No function in this package aborts the program.

### Access and return contracts

All parameters use shared access. No function requires mutable access. All return values stay fresh. `timestamp_from_iso_string` returns through `Error!` on text outside the accepted ISO format. `to_iso_string` returns through `Error!` when the instant lies outside the renderable range. Every other function stays infallible.

### Backend behaviour

HTML-JS lowers `mark_now` to `globalThis.performance.now()`. `timestamp_now` lowers to `Date.now()`. `to_iso_string` lowers to a validating runtime helper. Duration and timestamp construction helpers lower to inline JavaScript expressions.

HTML-Wasm lacks a Wasm lowering for these functions. See @../../../progress (progress matrix) for current target availability.

### Unsupported source forms

- Direct symbol-path clauses such as `@core/time/mark_now` face rejection. Use direct selections or a namespace clause.
- Named arguments are not supported. Binding-backed calls are positional-only.
- Do not construct `Duration`, `TimeMark` or `Timestamp` with struct syntax.
- Do not inspect opaque type fields directly.
- The package does not expose receiver methods.

### Deferred surfaces

Calendar date types, time zones, local-time conversion, locale-aware formatting, timers, sleep, intervals, animation callbacks and non-JS lowerings remain deferred. Browser animation-frame integration belongs in a future web-specific package rather than `@core/time`. See @../../../progress (progress matrix) for current status.

### Related concepts

- For package origin and backing see @../../#package-origins-and-backing (Package origins and backing).
- For dependency-path rules see @../../#dependency-paths (Dependency paths).
